{
 "title": "Methods and impl",
 "blocks": [
  {
   "t": "p",
   "text": "To add behaviour to a type you write an **`impl`** block. The first parameter of a method is `self`; you reach the object's fields with `self.field`. A call looks like `object.method()`."
  },
  {
   "t": "code",
   "code": [
    "struct Rectangle:",
    "    width: f64",
    "    height: f64",
    "",
    "impl Rectangle:",
    "    fn area(self) -> f64:",
    "        return self.width * self.height",
    "",
    "fn main():",
    "    rect = Rectangle{ width: 10.0, height: 5.0 }",
    "    print(\"Area:\", rect.area())"
   ],
   "run": true,
   "output": [
    "Area: 50"
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "p",
   "text": "A method that changes the object in place writes `self: lend Type` (you will learn `lend` in chapter 5: \"borrow and change\"). Methods that take no `self` are **static** methods and are often used as constructors: `Type.name(...)`."
  },
  {
   "t": "code",
   "code": [
    "struct Player:",
    "    name: str",
    "    health: i32",
    "",
    "impl Player:",
    "    fn new(name: str) -> Player:",
    "        return Player{ name: name, health: 100 }",
    "",
    "    fn is_alive(self) -> bool:",
    "        return self.health > 0",
    "",
    "    fn take_damage(self: lend Player, amount: i32):",
    "        self.health -= amount",
    "        if self.health < 0:",
    "            self.health = 0",
    "",
    "fn main():",
    "    hero = Player.new(\"Aragorn\")",
    "    hero.take_damage(35)",
    "    print(\"Health:\", hero.health)",
    "    print(\"Alive?\", hero.is_alive())"
   ],
   "run": true,
   "output": [
    "Health: 65",
    "Alive? true"
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "exercise",
   "id": "c04-l02-x1",
   "prompt": "Add an `increment` method to the `Counter` struct (`value: i32`): it increases `value` by 1. Call it three times and print `value`.",
   "starter": [
    "struct Counter:",
    "    value: i32",
    "",
    "impl Counter:",
    "    fn increment(self: lend Counter):",
    "        pass",
    "",
    "fn main():",
    "    c = Counter{ value: 0 }",
    "    c.increment()",
    "    c.increment()",
    "    c.increment()",
    "    print(c.value)"
   ],
   "solution": [
    "struct Counter:",
    "    value: i32",
    "",
    "impl Counter:",
    "    fn increment(self: lend Counter):",
    "        self.value += 1",
    "",
    "fn main():",
    "    c = Counter{ value: 0 }",
    "    c.increment()",
    "    c.increment()",
    "    c.increment()",
    "    print(c.value)"
   ],
   "hint": "Replace `pass` with `self.value += 1`.",
   "expected": [
    "3"
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "quiz",
   "id": "c04-l02-q1",
   "q": "What is a method without `self` called?",
   "options": [
    "Private method",
    "Static method (often a constructor)",
    "Broken method"
   ],
   "answer": 1,
   "why": "Methods called like `Player.new(...)` are static."
  }
 ]
}
